{"@type":"Question","name":"What is final expense insurance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Final expense is a small whole life insurance policy — usually $10,000 to $25,000 in face amount — designed to cover funeral, burial, and other end-of-life costs. Premiums are level and never change, the coverage doesn't expire, and the death benefit pays out tax-free, typically within a couple of weeks of a filed claim."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the first question to ask a final expense agent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ask which underwriting class you are being placed in: Preferred, Standard, Graded Benefit, or Guaranteed Issue. The class can change your premium by 50% or more for the same face amount. A common bad-faith move is selling Guaranteed Issue to people who could qualify for Preferred or Standard just because the application is easier."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the nonforfeiture trap in final expense policies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Many whole life policies default the nonforfeiture option to Extended Term Insurance, which converts your cash value into term coverage that eventually expires — defeating the point of permanent insurance. For most final expense buyers, Reduced Paid-Up Insurance is the better choice and should be set during the application."}}

replacements.new:
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is final expense insurance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Final expense insurance is a small whole life policy meant to cover funeral, burial or cremation costs and final bills. You choose the face amount. On a whole life final expense policy the premium is usually level, and the coverage lasts for life as long as you pay. Under IRS rules the death benefit is generally not taxable income to your beneficiary; interest paid on it is taxable. Graded and guaranteed-issue policies may pay only a reduced amount, such as the premiums paid, if death comes in the early years, often the first 2 or 3; the exact period is in the policy."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What's the first question to ask a final expense agent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ask which underwriting class you're being placed in: Preferred, Standard, Graded Benefit, or Guaranteed Issue (names vary by insurer). The class can change your premium a great deal for the same face amount, and the graded and guaranteed-issue classes limit what your family receives in the early years. A bad-faith move is selling Guaranteed Issue to people who could qualify for Preferred or Standard just because the application is easier."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the nonforfeiture trap?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you stop paying, the policy uses the nonforfeiture option you chose, or its default if you didn't choose one. On some policies the default is Extended Term Insurance, which turns your cash value into term coverage that ends after a set time. Reduced Paid-Up Insurance keeps a smaller benefit for life, with no more premiums due. Ask which option your policy uses and whether you can choose Reduced Paid-Up on the application."}}
